      "created_at": "2019-11-22T11:11:18Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-11-22T11:11:18Z",
      "body": "Is it often the case in DBL's situation that the uploader may be a different person than the person who produced the bundle?  I'm wondering whether it might perhaps be sufficient for DBL to record the uploader name alongside the bundle based on their authentication credentials.\r\n\r\nFor the record, GDPR is quite broad when it determines the meaning of personal data; even including the hair colour of the burrito producer would already become tricky.  Identifiers fall under it because they indirectly refer to a personal identity and can be used to track that individual.  Article 4 is unambiguous about this (emphasis mine):\r\n> ‘Personal data’ means any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person (‘data subject’); an identifiable natural person is one who can be identified, **directly or indirectly**, in particular by reference to an identifier such as a name, an **identification number**, location data, an **online identifier** or to one or more factors specific to the physical, physiological, genetic, mental, economic, cultural or social identity of that natural person.\r\n\r\nThis would mean that burritos do fall under data protection laws, and anyone processing burritos containing references to EU citizens and sharing them with third parties would have to abide by the stipulations of the GDPR, such as requiring explicit (and withdrawable) consent to share them with specifically enumerated third-party recipients of the data for specifically enumerated purposes.\r\n\r\nThis is not the responsibility of the SBWG but of whoever produces and processes burritos, so we could simply make these fields optional and not concern ourselves further with it, but these considerations may add a practical barrier to the usefulness of this field.",
      "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
